## Why This Is Asked
This question probes cross-region consistency, edge-scale reliability, and clear runbooks for failures.

## Key Concepts
- Distributed consensus with multi-DC Raft and quorum tuning
- Partition tolerance and per-tenant sharding
- Idempotent, sequenced operations
- Anti-entropy reconciliation and safe replay
- Observability, alerting, and rollback runbooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// simple idempotent write with sequence tokens
function applyWrite(store, key, value, token) {
  const cur = store[key];
  if (!cur || token > cur.token) {
    store[key] = { value, token };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the reconciliation path under partitions?
- What metrics signal reconciliation backlog and potential data loss?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design scalable, tenant-aware incident response and degradation at scale. It tests how candidates balance automated safeguards with precise human oversight and how runbooks integrate metrics, backfills, and recovery verification.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region, multi-tenant SLAs
- Degradation strategies and traffic shaping
- Cache strategy and data consistency during outages
- Canary testing, rollback criteria, post-incident validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo per-tenant degradation switch
function route(request){
  const t = request.tenant;
  if (t.degradation === 'high') return routeToCacheOnly();
  return routeToFullPath();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate this runbook in a synthetic outage? 
- Which dashboards and alerting thresholds would you use for tenants at risk?
